Core scope

reading and creating patterns;
grain, direction, stretch, and material behaviour;
seam allowance and construction sequence;
hand and machine sewing foundations;
closures and fasteners;
embroidery and heat-transfer processes;
wearable, protective, storage, and other soft-goods design;
fit prototypes, user testing, repair, and resource-efficient layout.

Required evidence

situation, user, need, requirements, and restrictions;
measurements and the source of critical dimensions;
alternatives and justification for using 3D printing;
editable CAD model and exported manufacturing file;
orientation and slicer decisions with a toolpath preview;
a fit or feature test where uncertainty is high;
observations from realistic use;
at least one model revision tied to evidence.

5. Build evidence before the full product
Create the smallest useful test first. This might be one opening, slot series, hook, magnet pocket, label system, removable joint, or cardboard reach mock-up.
Test and record:

fit and clearance using the actual tools;
removal and return time;
one-hand access and neighbouring-tool collisions;
visibility and missing-tool recognition;
stability, mounting, and expected load direction;
assembly time, part count, material use, and estimated cost;
user words, actions, and emotional response;
one change made because of the evidence.

Do not claim a safe load, accessibility, durability, or ergonomic success without a suitable test.

6. Submit the design story
Your submission must include:

a precedent board with at least three attributed references;
a feature-and-problem analysis in your own words;
a measured improvement brief with criteria and constraints;
three concepts and a decision matrix;
your own editable CAD or vector source and an exported fabrication file;
a critical-dimension coupon or low-fidelity prototype;
build photographs and a short user test;
a before-and-after comparison against the chosen precedent;
revision notes showing what changed and why;
a final evaluation that distinguishes measured results from opinions and unverified claims.

Mesh checks

use transformations deliberately and apply scale when the workflow requires it;
inspect normals, intersections, holes and extremely thin regions;
keep source files editable and name important objects;
use only original or appropriately licensed textures, models and reference images;
distinguish a visual render from evidence that geometry is manufacturable.

Next: Small Website & UI/UX Portfolio.

Layout audit
Take a crowded sign, instruction sheet, or interface and identify:

the intended first action;
competing elements;
weak or inconsistent alignment;
text that can be shortened;
groups that need more or less spacing.

Create a revised version and test it with someone who has not seen the original.

Ontario curriculum relevance: Useful for TGJ2O and senior Communications Technology, TDJ2O, and TIJ1O work involving graphic communication, technical documentation, signage, websites, interfaces, and presentation of design ideas.

Next: Animation, UI/UX & Interface Prototypes.

Generate alternatives
Use quick sketches, shape combinations, material changes, alternate orientations, and “borrow–combine–adapt–improve” prompts. At this stage, quantity creates options; evaluation comes next.
For each alternative, identify:

the requirement it addresses;
the restriction it respects;
what makes it meaningfully different;
one uncertainty that needs a test.

Pitch the concept
A useful pitch package may include:

a short statement connecting the situation, need, and proposed response;
a labelled sketch or digital drawing;
critical dimensions and materials;
a user flow, exploded view, sequence, or interface state where appropriate;
the decision criteria used to select the concept;
a specific question for feedback.

Communication is not decoration. The pitch should explain the situation, need, proposal, value, evidence, and the specific decisions still open to feedback. It tests whether the idea is coherent and whether other people can identify gaps before more time and material are spent.

Why SVG matters
SVG is an open, text-based vector format that can preserve document dimensions, paths, groups, fills, and strokes. It can move between tools, but import settings still matter. Students should verify:

document units are millimetres when physical size matters;
a known rectangle measures correctly after export and import;
text is available in the destination environment or converted to paths in a copy;
the software distinguishes filled artwork from vector paths;
repeated or overlapping cut lines have been removed.

Beginner tool: Pencil2D
Pencil2D is a focused open-source tool for hand-drawn raster and vector animation. Keep the first task small enough to revise:

storyboard a start, change and return;
create the main keyframes first;
turn on onion skinning and add only the in-between frames needed;
preview the loop at different timings;
hold important poses longer instead of redrawing them repeatedly;
export using the teacher-approved format and reopen the export.

Name the audience, their goal and the shortest useful path through the page. UI is the visible controls and presentation; UX is the larger experience of understanding, acting, receiving feedback and recovering from mistakes.
Plan:

hierarchy: what should be noticed first, second and third;
layout: related items group together with usable spacing;
navigation: links are labelled clearly and the current location is understandable;
states and feedback: selected, loading, success, empty and error where relevant;
responsive thinking: content reflows and remains readable on a phone;
files and paths: image filenames, folders and links remain valid after publishing;
accessibility: semantic headings, meaningful alternative text, keyboard access, contrast and readable motion.

