Learning goals
By the end, you can:

explain what a raster image is;
choose between JPG, PNG, WebP, and SVG for a specific job;
recognize transparency and explain why it matters;
create an 11 × 4 inch canvas at 300 pixels per inch;
use guides to create a 0.25 inch safe margin;
arrange a logo and name with readable typography, balanced spacing, and clear hierarchy;
save an editable master and export a checked PNG for engraving.

Prepare the design

simplify the SVG and remove accidental overlaps, stray nodes and duplicate lines;
set the document units and intended physical scale;
check antlers, points, bridges and other small features against the actual material/process;
make the hanging hole large enough for the approved string or hook and leave enough material around it;
separate CUT geometry from ENGRAVE artwork using the local file convention;
add original personalization without weakening critical regions;
print at full size or cut a small approved test before producing the final ornament.

First task
Draw front, top and side views. Mark:

plaque size and thickness;
support footprint;
slot or fastener;
likely tipping direction;
one dimension that needs a test.

A good stand

stays upright after a light desk bump;
keeps centre of mass over its support area;
does not hide the engraving;
assembles without forcing parts;
records what changed after the cardboard or scrap test.

First task
Measure three examples of the object. Record:

widest controlling dimension;
range of measurements;
removal clearance;
centre-to-centre spacing;
remaining material at the edge and between openings.

Ready to fabricate?

measured bottle parameters replaced defaults;
BOM matches visible parts;
assembly order is possible;
fit coupon passed;
free-standing stability or wall plan is approved;
file still says unverified until the physical record exists.

Evidence, not decoration
Hand in:

paper planning sheet;
editable SVG master;
fabrication/export file;
BOM or part list;
fit/stability evidence;
one clear revision statement.

Test the interface
Create a small coupon containing several clearances, hole diameters, or pin/socket pairs around the target dimension. Label each value in the model. Print with the intended printer, material, orientation, and profile; measure the result and choose a fit from evidence.
Functional choices include:

free-running clearance for moving or removable parts;
sliding fit for guided assembly;
snug fit for repeatable location;
press fit where material behaviour and removal requirements make it appropriate;
pilot, clearance, counterbore, or captured-nut features for conventional fasteners.

Evidence to submit

original sketches and source/licence notes;
criteria and overall dimensions;
Onshape source/version plus exported STL or 3MF;
screenshot of constrained sketch and feature history;
slicer preview and orientation justification;
first print with observations;
finishing/colour process notes;
final evaluation and source-model revision.







NoteWearable does not mean automatically safe



Teacher approval is required for attachment hardware and finishing materials. Remove sharp edges and fragile projections. Do not use a classroom prototype where failure, ingestion, skin sensitivity or entanglement could create a hazard.


Review Slicing, Orientation & Supports and Tolerances & Functional Parts as needed.

Learning sequence

CNC router and CNC mill capabilities and limits;
machine coordinates, work coordinates, and zero/reference choices;
secure workholding and clearance planning;
end-mill geometry and tool selection;
CAM setup, pockets, profiles, drilling, and toolpath order;
feeds, speeds, chip formation, and machine/material-specific evidence;
simulation, dry-run practices, verification, inspection, and revision.

Machine-specific safety instruction follows school board guidelines and the course information provided by the teacher. Students in Mr. Andrade’s classes use Google Classroom for that material.

Additional methods

vinyl cutting and applied graphics;
plasma cutting and thermal-cutting workflows;
sheet-metal layout, cutting, bending, and fastening;
vacuum forming and mold design;
molding and casting;
composite layup and finishing;
hybrid projects that choose several processes for different functions.

Each method should begin with the N.I.C.E. design process, explain what the process is suited to, distinguish digital geometry from machine setup, and require testing rather than presenting generic settings as universal.

Do not grade an answer by confidence, length or formatting. Annotate it:

supported: verified by a measurement, original source or local instruction;
uncertain: plausible but not yet checked;
unsupported: no usable evidence or citation;
unsafe/out of scope: conflicts with approved materials, tools, supervision or the assignment;
missing: ignores an important user, accessibility, maintenance, assembly or fabrication constraint.

Ask whose needs and bodies the proposed design assumes. Check reach, grip, contrast, language, one-handed use, cost and access. Look for stereotypes or a “typical user” who was never defined.

